Z||000003||Jay||08-22-2001||10:02 PM||||One  word of   advice.  Instead of investing in a custom harness to work with the stock ECM check out getting an after market stand alone ECM.  Won't look for ABS, Traction Control ect.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Sleeper SE||08-22-2001||10:06 PM||||what mods did the fiero shop do to the cradle<BR>?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sleeper SE||08-22-2001||10:08 PM||||Jay would howel be a sorce for such a n ecm?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jay||08-22-2001||10:20 PM||||Fiero shop in Toronto welded and modified the cradle for the automatic transmission and for mounting spots for the N*.  It's solid mounted.  The cross member on the cradle (closest to the fire wall) was cut right out and a new beam welded further back and higher up.  Sorry forgot to take pics of this.  Howel doesn't have the ECM but Haltech makes one that will work...or so I am told, and it is programmable via lap top.  You will need a dyno to program it correctly.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sleeper SE||08-22-2001||10:27 PM||||Thanks again for the info. got a laptop but no dyno. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Formula88||08-23-2001||10:13 AM||||Check out <A HREF="http://www.chrfab.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.chrfab.com</A>  . They sell all sorts of N* hot rodding equipment, including ECM's and ignition systems.  Not cheap, but they appear to be high quality.||||reg||6
Z||000009||artherd||08-23-2001||02:06 PM||||For $1300 bux, the guys at Cadilac Hotrod Fabricators will sell you a Holley 950 Commander ECU, *and* a *full* wiring harness that is totally plug and play.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.chrfab.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.chrfab.com</A> <P>I will have mine soon, so I will post a full report then!<P>Hey Jay, could you send me a pic of your front engine mount sometime?  Also, did you mount the entire motor to the cradle alone, or use  a solid strut or two up top?  I can't decide if I should use an upper mount as well (don't want to  warp my cradle :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||Jay||08-23-2001||05:30 PM||||My engine is solid mounted to the cradle, no bushings and no dog bones up top.  It is smooth as silk, no vibrations and has been totally reliable for the past year and a half.  Sorry no pics of the cradle, one of the only things I don't have pics of.<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR>1986 Northstar GT<BR>www.members.home.net/jaygrande<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cayuga3_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||08-24-2001||11:50 AM||||Coolness!  Looks like I'm going to go that route!<P>If you ever do find yourself under your car with the wheels off, and you just have this camera there and you don't know why, before you scream, might you snap me one someday? <P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jay:<BR><B>My engine is solid mounted to the cradle, no bushings and no dog bones up top.  It is smooth as silk, no vibrations and has been totally reliable for the past year and a half.  Sorry no pics of the cradle, one of the only things I don't have pics of.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
